private network shared with node2 and node3\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 (VyOS) eth1 was configured with static address 10.50.50.254/24 and a Kea DHCP server (shared-network LAN, subnet 10.50.50.0/24, range .100-.200, default-router/name-server options, 3600s lease) was committed and saved to config.boot. The running kea-dhcp4.conf and process (ps aux) confirm the service is live, and after-executor listening-sockets show kea-dhcp4 bound to 10.50.50.254:67. Both intended DHCP clients successfully received leases from this server, directly confirming the server is functioning on the shared network.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8100ac44f3484119\",\"cmd-f205aef7d2224185\",\"cmd-8706a091d0ec46a8\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"node2-dhcp-client\",\"requirement\":\"node2 configured to obtain its private-network address from node1 via DHCP instead of static assignment\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"/etc/network/interfaces on node2 was changed to 'iface eth1 inet dhcp'; networking restart logs show dhcpcd soliciting and receiving a lease (10.50.50.100) explicitly 'from 10.50.50.254' (node1). Subsequent ip addr/route output confirms the dynamic-scope address and a default route via node1. node1's lease database independently recorded this lease under hostname 'node2'.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8f6829db18a647e7\",\"cmd-8a267e1f666d4187\",\"cmd-e4e7a5b49e614fb6\"]},{\"id\":\"node3-dhcp-client\",\"requirement\":\"node3 configured to obtain its private-network address from node1 via DHCP instead of static assignment\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Same change applied to node3: /etc/network/interfaces set eth1 to dhcp; dhcpcd log shows lease 10.50.50.101 offered/leased 'from 10.50.50.254'; ip addr/route confirms dynamic address and default route via node1; node1's lease CSV independently recorded the lease under hostname 'node3'.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-994013818f1e44c2\",\"cmd-f73e0c5ef4244cba\",\"cmd-e4e7a5b49e614fb6\"]},{\"id\":\"provider-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all managed nodes with pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/config state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show a complete bounded-disruption transition: pre-restart boot_id capture, an explicit reboot command, a period of connection failures consistent with the node being down, then a new boot_id, followed by direct recovery checks. node1's changed boot_id (36437d9e) and recovered eth1 address, running kea-dhcp4 process, intact lease file and persisted config.boot match the fixed after-executor snapshot. node2 and node3 changed boot_ids (20a60135, 310f1fc4) match their after-executor snapshots, and both automatically re-acquired their prior DHCP-assigned addresses (10.50.50.100/.101) from node1 on boot, confirming the DHCP-client configuration is itself persistent. A final check after all three reboots shows node1's kea-dhcp4 still running with fresh renewed leases from both clients, confirming end-to-end service continuity.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f8579187fb584408\",\"cmd-99ede62094b342ac\",\"cmd-a30abe28fb3f4059\",\"cmd-de0c4d6cc0c94011\",\"cmd-70721e701ea846b5\",\"cmd-0fdb6a4901fd4191\",\"cmd-6d2461ce43dd4e1b\",\"cmd-357f1c81ee354006\",\"cmd-7ff68593772f444a\",\"cmd-65e8c3a67c7d4923\",\"cmd-8ea771fa149b4fac\",\"cmd-cf6c93b428e646e8\",\"cmd-8095508c1d28468e\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"The command trace is largely composed of read-only discovery (find/grep/cat of VyOS templates and config, ip/link inspection) needed to locate the current Kea-based dhcp-server CLI syntax after legacy isc-dhcp-style syntax failed, plus the minimal state changes required to satisfy the task: bringing up eth1 on node2/node3 (cmd-958abbad28294280, cmd-ff5323f2ce48459d), rewriting /etc/network/interfaces on both Alpine nodes (cmd-8f6829db18a647e7, cmd-994013818f1e44c2), and configuring/committing the DHCP server on node1. One state-changing command, cmd-44ea90c00f914f7c, attempted an invalid dhcp-server configuration (subnet-parameters/default-router/name-server at the wrong level) which failed at commit ('Commit failed... uncommitted changes that will not be saved'); since VyOS discards uncommitted edits on session exit and the following successful commit (cmd-8100ac44f3484119) used corrected syntax, this failed attempt left no residue but is counted as a bounded, self-corrected troubleshooting mutation. The three sequential single-node reboots were necessary to satisfy the independent restart-evidence protocol. Post-task snapshots show no failed services, no unexpected package-health issues (only a pre-existing chrony.drift diff on Alpine nodes carried over unchanged from before-prepare), and only expected residue (per-boot systemd-private tmp dirs, /tmp/reboot.log from the reboot helper script) with no unexplained leftover files or exposed services beyond the intended DHCP server.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-44ea90c00f914f7c\",\"cmd-8100ac44f3484119\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material contract requirements are satisfied with direct evidence: node1 runs a committed, persisted Kea DHCP server on the shared 10.50.50.0/24 network, and node2/node3 were reconfigured from static to DHCP on eth1, both successfully leasing addresses from node1 (confirmed independently via client-side logs/ip output and server-side lease-database records). The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also executed cleanly on all three nodes, with baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and full recovery of network, DHCP-server, and lease state directly observed and cross-checked against the fixed after-executor snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"One node1 configuration attempt (cmd-44ea90c00f914f7c) used invalid CLI syntax and failed at commit; the mechanism by which the eth1 static address later appeared applied (despite this failed, allegedly-unsaved commit) is not fully explained by the captured trace, though the final committed config.boot and running state are self-consistent and correct.\",\"Kea's interfaces-config uses the VyOS-generated default 'interfaces: [\\\"*\\\"]', so the DHCP server technically listens on all interfaces (including the mgmt network) rather than being scoped to eth1 only; this is standard VyOS template behavior rather than an executor misconfiguration, and no evidence shows it caused any unintended lease assignment on the mgmt network.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"node1-dhcp-server","requirement":"node1 configured to run a DHCP server on the private network shared with node2 and node3","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 (VyOS) eth1 was configured with static address 10.50.50.254/24 and a Kea DHCP server (shared-network LAN, subnet 10.50.50.0/24, range .100-.200, default-router/name-server options, 3600s lease) was committed and saved to config.boot. The running kea-dhcp4.conf and process (ps aux) confirm the service is live, and after-executor listening-sockets show kea-dhcp4 bound to 10.50.50.254:67. Both intended DHCP clients successfully received leases from this server, directly confirming the server is functioning on the shared network.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8100ac44f3484119","cmd-f205aef7d2224185","cmd-8706a091d0ec46a8","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"node2-dhcp-client","requirement":"node2 configured to obtain its private-network address from node1 via DHCP instead of static assignment","status":"satisfied","summary":"/etc/network/interfaces on node2 was changed to 'iface eth1 inet dhcp'; networking restart logs show dhcpcd soliciting and receiving a lease (10.50.50.100) explicitly 'from 10.50.50.254' (node1). Subsequent ip addr/route output confirms the dynamic-scope address and a default route via node1. node1's lease database independently recorded this lease under hostname 'node2'.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8f6829db18a647e7","cmd-8a267e1f666d4187","cmd-e4e7a5b49e614fb6"]},{"id":"node3-dhcp-client","requirement":"node3 configured to obtain its private-network address from node1 via DHCP instead of static assignment","status":"satisfied","summary":"Same change applied to node3: /etc/network/interfaces set eth1 to dhcp; dhcpcd log shows lease 10.50.50.101 offered/leased 'from 10.50.50.254'; ip addr/route confirms dynamic address and default route via node1; node1's lease CSV independently recorded the lease under hostname 'node3'.","evidence_ids":["cmd-994013818f1e44c2","cmd-f73e0c5ef4244cba","cmd-e4e7a5b49e614fb6"]},{"id":"provider-restart-protocol","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all managed nodes with pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/config state","status":"satisfied","summary":"All three nodes show a complete bounded-disruption transition: pre-restart boot_id capture, an explicit reboot command, a period of connection failures consistent with the node being down, then a new boot_id, followed by direct recovery checks. node1's changed boot_id (36437d9e) and recovered eth1 address, running kea-dhcp4 process, intact lease file and persisted config.boot match the fixed after-executor snapshot. node2 and node3 changed boot_ids (20a60135, 310f1fc4) match their after-executor snapshots, and both automatically re-acquired their prior DHCP-assigned addresses (10.50.50.100/.101) from node1 on boot, confirming the DHCP-client configuration is itself persistent. A final check after all three reboots shows node1's kea-dhcp4 still running with fresh renewed leases from both clients, confirming end-to-end service continuity.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f8579187fb584408","cmd-99ede62094b342ac","cmd-a30abe28fb3f4059","cmd-de0c4d6cc0c94011","cmd-70721e701ea846b5","cmd-0fdb6a4901fd4191","cmd-6d2461ce43dd4e1b","cmd-357f1c81ee354006","cmd-7ff68593772f444a","cmd-65e8c3a67c7d4923","cmd-8ea771fa149b4fac","cmd-cf6c93b428e646e8","cmd-8095508c1d28468e","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"The command trace is largely composed of read-only discovery (find/grep/cat of VyOS templates and config, ip/link inspection) needed to locate the current Kea-based dhcp-server CLI syntax after legacy isc-dhcp-style syntax failed, plus the minimal state changes required to satisfy the task: bringing up eth1 on node2/node3 (cmd-958abbad28294280, cmd-ff5323f2ce48459d), rewriting /etc/network/interfaces on both Alpine nodes (cmd-8f6829db18a647e7, cmd-994013818f1e44c2), and configuring/committing the DHCP server on node1. One state-changing command, cmd-44ea90c00f914f7c, attempted an invalid dhcp-server configuration (subnet-parameters/default-router/name-server at the wrong level) which failed at commit ('Commit failed... uncommitted changes that will not be saved'); since VyOS discards uncommitted edits on session exit and the following successful commit (cmd-8100ac44f3484119) used corrected syntax, this failed attempt left no residue but is counted as a bounded, self-corrected troubleshooting mutation. The three sequential single-node reboots were necessary to satisfy the independent restart-evidence protocol. Post-task snapshots show no failed services, no unexpected package-health issues (only a pre-existing chrony.drift diff on Alpine nodes carried over unchanged from before-prepare), and only expected residue (per-boot systemd-private tmp dirs, /tmp/reboot.log from the reboot helper script) with no unexplained leftover files or exposed services beyond the intended DHCP server.","evidence_ids":["cmd-44ea90c00f914f7c","cmd-8100ac44f3484119","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material contract requirements are satisfied with direct evidence: node1 runs a committed, persisted Kea DHCP server on the shared 10.50.50.0/24 network, and node2/node3 were reconfigured from static to DHCP on eth1, both successfully leasing addresses from node1 (confirmed independently via client-side logs/ip output and server-side lease-database records). The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also executed cleanly on all three nodes, with baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and full recovery of network, DHCP-server, and lease state directly observed and cross-checked against the fixed after-executor snapshots.","limitations":["One node1 configuration attempt (cmd-44ea90c00f914f7c) used invalid CLI syntax and failed at commit; the mechanism by which the eth1 static address later appeared applied (despite this failed, allegedly-unsaved commit) is not fully explained by the captured trace, though the final committed config.boot and running state are self-consistent and correct.","Kea's interfaces-config uses the VyOS-generated default 'interfaces: [\"*\"]', so the DHCP server technically listens on all interfaces (including the mgmt network) rather than being scoped to eth1 only; this is standard VyOS template behavior rather than an executor misconfiguration, and no evidence shows it caused any unintended lease assignment on the mgmt network."]},"ttft_ms":48089,"type":"result","duration_ms":99997,"uuid":"29a34eb6-f4e8-479e-af6b-f4b3f7208e96","ttft_stream_ms":2440,"time_to_request_ms":51,"queued_turn_count":0}
